import std/io

// A USER's own interface whose NAME is a stdlib interface's name.
//
// `ToJson` is declared as `pub interface ToJson` in std/json.nomi. It is a real
// stdlib interface and it is NOT one of the 13 the prelude auto-prepends, so
// this file declares it without importing anything and nothing is in scope to
// collide with. A stdlib interface NAME is therefore not what makes something a
// stdlib interface — only the declaring module is, which is the whole content of
// the identity rule in stdiface.go.
//
// This fixture was written against `Display` on the measured ground that the
// analyzer accepted `pub interface Display`, because checkReservedTypeName
// reached defineStructStub / defineEnumStub / defineTypeDefStub /
// defineTypeAliasStub and never defineInterfaceStub. That acceptance was the
// DEFECT, not the language: it made `where T: Display` pass statically for a
// type with no `to_string` and the program crash at runtime. Prelude interface
// names are now reserved (analysis/builder.go), so the `Display` spelling is
// rejected at its declaration and the name-keyed hazard is FENCED rather than
// merely unobserved. analysis/reserved_names_test.go pins the fence; this file
// keeps the builder half reachable through a name the fence does not cover.
//
// What must happen: this declaration wins, its dispatch table is a variable in
// THIS file's generated package, and rt's shared stdlib-interface tables are not
// touched. A registry keyed on the NAME would instead bind these impls into rt's
// table, a wrong answer rather than a
// coverage gap, and one no corpus program can see because none declares one.
pub interface ToJson {
    fn to_string(value: self): String
}

struct Mine {
    n: Int
}

struct Other {
    n: Int
}

impl ToJson for Mine {
    fn to_string(m: Mine): String {
        "mine(${m.n})"
    }
}

impl ToJson for Other {
    fn to_string(o: Other): String {
        "mine(${o.n})"
    }
}

fn show(d: ToJson): String {
    ToJson.to_string(d)
}

fn main() {
    io.print(show(Mine{n: 1}))
    io.print(show(Other{n: 2}))
}
